{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01465NoWAgTP8BKQZPTa6sct","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's the full content of `cmd/sponge/main.go`. This is an excellent reference implementation for your style guide:\n\n**Key features for style reference:**\n\n1. **Package header and import organization** - Clean copyright/license header, followed by package docstring, then organized imports with external packages at the end.\n\n2. **Flag parsing** - Simple `flag.Bool` for the `-a` append mode flag at the package level.\n\n3. **Error handling pattern** - Consistent `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"sponge: %v\\n\", err)` for error messages, and functions return `(int, error)` or just `int` for exit codes.\n\n4. **Main structure** - `main()` calls `run()` which does the actual work and returns an exit code, with signal handling and cleanup via `defer`.\n\n5. **Helper functions** - Well-factored helper functions like `readAllStdin()`, `outputFileInfo()`, `writeOutputFile()` that each do one thing.\n\n6. **Signal handling** - Uses `signal.Notify()` with a goroutine to handle cleanup on interrupts (SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGPIPE).\n\n7. **File operations** - Demonstrates atomic writes with fallback strategies, proper error wrapping with `%w`, and temporary file management.\n\n8. **Sync primitives** - Uses `sync.Mutex` for thread-safe access to the temporary file path.\n\nThis is a good reference for how to structure a moreutils command with proper error handling, flag parsing, and cleanup logic."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68705226-65db-4758-9362-c780b1b078e9","uuid":"6a7a8849-908d-45a4-9b0a-45724a9c0f40","timestamp":"2026-05-28T16:50:13.267Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5631, I need to see a simple existing moreutils cmd/ implementation for style reference. Look for cmd/sponge/main.go, cm